mode: "100644"
text: "# The reality of text posts in 2026 (verify-quarterly)\n\nVolatile figures — attribute, mark **verify-quarterly**. The principles (the first line is most of the job; white\nspace + restraint win; structure beats length) are stable; the exact numbers/limits move.\n\n## The fold is everything\n- **The \"see more\" cutoff is a hard wall.** On LinkedIn it lands at roughly **~210 chars on desktop / ~140 on\n  mobile** (design for mobile); everything above it is the entire pitch, everything below needs a deliberate tap.\n  Treat the first ~2-3 lines as a **mini-post whose only job is to earn the click** — \"if those first sentences\n  don't compel the tap, the rest of your post doesn't exist.\" (attribute; verify-quarterly)\n- **Lead with the insight**, not \"I'm excited to announce…\", a greeting, or a bland question. Front-load the\n  strongest, most specific line.\n\n## White space + the 2026 over-formatting tell\n- **White space is a feature**, not wasted space — short 1-2 sentence paragraphs with line breaks reportedly lift\n  reading time by up to ~20% (ConnectSafely; attribute). Walls of text don't get read. \"Space = oxygen.\"\n- **OVER-formatting now backfires.** The biggest 2026 mistake isn't under-formatting — it's the\n  Unicode-bold-everywhere, emoji/checkmark-per-line, rigid \"Problem/Solution/Takeaway\" look, which reads as\n  **AI-generated** and gets scrolled past. Restraint (white space + a strong first line + at most one emphasis)\n  beats decoration. \"Formatting should make a genuine thought easier to read, not paper over a thin one.\"\n- Unicode styling isn't searchable and **breaks screen readers** — keep key terms/hashtags plain (accessibility).\n\n## Length: match content + platform (structure does the work)\n- **LinkedIn:** 3,000-char cap (held since June 2023); the engagement sweet spot is ~**1,300-1,900** for\n  thought-leadership/storytelling, **under ~500** for hot takes; posts over ~2,000-2,500 see a reported ~35%\n  engagement drop. **X** favors brevity; **Threads** ~500 chars; **Bluesky** ~300. (attribute; verify-quarterly)\n- A reader experiences \"a hook, a turn, a payoff, a CTA\" — **not** \"1,600 characters.\" Structure, not length,\n  does the work.\n\n## Engagement (comments/saves/dwell beat likes)\n- Comments + dwell time are the heaviest distribution signals; a 1,400-char post with real saves + substantive\n  comments beats an 1,800-char post with 50 likes and three \"🔥\" replies.\n- **The CTA must be a genuine, specific invitation** (\"What am I missing?\") — \"comment YES / like & share\" is\n  engagement-bait, now suppressed. **Native beats external links** (put links in comments on LinkedIn).\n- **Write to one specific reader** — a post for everyone resonates with no one.\n\n## House metrics\nSaves/comments/dwell + profile visits/follows + clicks — read natively; never fabricate a number or stuff bait.\n"
